---
layout: base.njk
permalink: /runs/index.html
title: Runs
---

<header class="detail-head">
	<h2 class="page-hed">Measurement runs</h2>
	<p class="lede">
		Every measurement pass, read from the NDJSON run log. When a number looks
		wrong, the first question is whether that run actually finished — this
		answers it without shell access to the build machine.
	</p>
</header>

{% if sites.recentlyMeasured.length %}
<section class="panel">
	{#
		The same rows as the stat bar's card on the home page — the link, the six
		band colors, the age — laid out across columns rather than down one.

		This page is about the measurement loop, so the list earns more room here
		than it gets in a card that has to share a scrolling row with five others.
		Columns rather than a longer single list: sixty rows in one column is a
		screen of scrolling to learn something a reader takes in at a glance.
	#}
	<h2>Recently measured <span class="tag">{{ sites.recentlyMeasured | length }} sites</span></h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Newest first, across the whole corpus. The dots are the four Lighthouse
		categories, then axe and Core Web Vitals, in the colors that run carried.
	</p>
	<ol class="recents recents-wide">
		{% for site in sites.recentlyMeasured %}
		<li>
			<a href="/site/{{ site.slug }}/">{{ site.displayUrl }}</a>
			<span class="recents-dots" aria-hidden="true">
				{% for band in site.bands %}<span class="recents-dot recents-dot-{{ band }}"></span>{% endfor %}
			</span>
			<span class="recents-age"><time datetime="{{ site.timestamp | iso }}">{{ site.timestamp | since }}</time></span>
		</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ol>
</section>
{% endif %}

{#
	The machines, before the runs on them.

	This belongs here rather than on a site page because it is a fact about the
	instrument, not about any site: every CPU-bound number published — TBT, the
	performance score, anything derived from them — was produced on one of these,
	and a run on a slow one reports a worse site having measured nothing
	different.
#}
{% if sites.benchmark %}
<section class="panel">
	<h2>The measuring machines <span class="tag">Lighthouse benchmark index</span></h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Recorded once per measurement, from the run whose numbers were kept.
		Higher is faster.
	</p>

	<dl class="bench-stats">
		<div>
			<dt>Median</dt>
			<dd>{{ sites.benchmark.median | num }}</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			{#
				Second, against the median it is drawn from: the pair reads as one
				statement about which machines count as ordinary here.
			#}
			<dt>&plusmn;{{ sites.benchmark.driftBand.pct }}% Median</dt>
			<dd>{{ sites.benchmark.driftBand.low | num }}&ndash;{{ sites.benchmark.driftBand.high | num }}</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Mean</dt>
			<dd>{{ sites.benchmark.mean | num }}</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Range</dt>
			<dd>{{ sites.benchmark.min | num }}&ndash;{{ sites.benchmark.max | num }}</dd>
		</div>
	</dl>

	<p class="muted small">
		{#
			Both averages, because they disagree: the mean sits above the median
			because a thin tail of fast runners pulls it there, while most runs
			cluster within a few percent of each other. Saying so is the difference
			between a statistic and a number.
		#}
		From {{ sites.benchmark.count | num }} {{ sites.benchmark.count | plural("measurement") }}.
		The mean sits above the median because a few unusually fast machines pull it
		there; most runs land close together. A site is told
		<em>&ldquo;the measuring machine was faster than most in this project&rdquo;</em>
		when its run lands outside that band.
		That is a distance rather than a percentile because the notice claims the
		CPU-bound numbers moved, and how far off the machine was decides that; how
		rare it was does not. These machines vary by running fast rather than slow,
		so in practice the notice is a fast-machine one.
	</p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if not runs.recent.length %}
<div class="empty">
	<p>No runs logged yet. Run <code>npm run measure</code> to create one.</p>
</div>
{% else %}

<section class="panel">
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-site">Started</th>
				<th>Duration</th>
				<th>Measured</th>
				<th>Skipped</th>
				<th>Failed</th>
				<th>Warnings</th>
				<th>Form factor</th>
				<th>Log file</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{% for run in runs.recent %}
			<tr{% if run.failed %} class="row-failing"{% endif %}>
				<td class="col-site">{{ run.startedAt | date("long") }}</td>
				<td class="num">{{ (run.durationMs / 1000) | round(1) }}s</td>
				<td class="num">{{ run.measured or run.written or 0 }}</td>
				<td class="num muted">{{ run.skipped or 0 }}</td>
				<td class="num {{ 'worse' if run.failed }}">{{ run.failed or 0 }}</td>
				<td class="num muted">{{ run.counts.warn or 0 }}</td>
				<td class="muted">{{ run.formFactor or "—" }}</td>
				<td class="muted"><code>{{ run.logFile }}</code></td>
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<h2>Reading the logs</h2>
	<p class="muted small">
		Each run writes a full NDJSON log to <code>logs/&lt;runId&gt;.ndjson</code>,
		one JSON object per line, plus a summary line appended to
		<code>logs/runs.ndjson</code>. Both are greppable:
	</p>
	<pre><code># Every warning and error across all runs
grep -h '"level":"warn"\|"level":"error"' logs/*.ndjson | jq .

# What happened to one site in one run
jq 'select(.data.url == "https://example.com/")' logs/2026-01-01*.ndjson

# Run history at a glance
npx speedlify runs</code></pre>
</section>

{% endif %}
